INT. RIVERBOAT CASINO - PRIVATE DINING SALON - NIGHT

Red velvet. Brass lamps. A paddle wheel churns beyond the portholes, and the whole room sways with the current.

A long table set for three, with a court reporter's absence conspicuous. At its head sits HORACE VANDERMEER (50s, silver temples, a gray double-breasted suit, a fountain pen he never uncaps). He is the attorney for the plaintiff.

Across from him, CORNELIUS "NEELY" ABBOTT (60s, ruddy, a carnation in his lapel, a glass of bourbon sweating at his elbow). The witness. Genial. Rich.

At the foot of the table, MILES KOWALCZYK (30s, lean, perfect posture, a fresh haircut) arranges his notes. He represents Abbott.

A deposition, held on a boat so no one can serve a subpoena in the wrong county.

HORACE
We'll keep this informal, Mr. Abbott.

NEELY
Neely. Everyone calls me Neely.

MILES
Mr. Abbott will answer to his name.

NEELY
Miles, relax. We're among friends.

HORACE
Are we?

NEELY
I hope so.

Horace smiles. Uncaps nothing. Turns a page.

HORACE
You've owned the Gilded Heron for how long?

NEELY
Eleven years. Since my father passed.

MILES
Objection. Relevance.

HORACE
It's a deposition, Mr. Kowalczyk. Objections are noted, not ruled upon.

MILES
Noted, then.

NEELY
Let him ask. I've nothing to hide.

A waiter pours coffee, withdraws. The paddle wheel thuds. Glasses shiver.

HORACE
And the vessel carries how many guests on a Friday?

NEELY
Three hundred, maybe three-fifty.

HORACE
Sprinklers?

NEELY
Of course.

HORACE
Inspected?

NEELY
Every year.

MILES
Mr. Abbott, you needn't elaborate.

NEELY
He's only asking about the boat.

HORACE
Only about the boat.

He turns another page. Pleasant. Unhurried. He might be reading a menu.

HORACE (CONT'D)
Wonderful. Then let's talk about the fire.

Neely's glass stops halfway to his lips.

NEELY
Which fire?

HORACE
The one in the engine room, August third.

NEELY
That was minor.

MILES
Mr. Abbott -

NEELY
- a small electrical matter. The crew handled it.

HORACE
The crew locked it.

NEELY
I don't follow.

HORACE
The engine room door. Locked from the outside. Eleven men inside.

MILES
Objection! He's testifying.

HORACE
He is, in fact, answering. Mr. Abbott?

NEELY
(too fast)
That door sticks. It's old.

HORACE
It sticks from the outside only?

NEELY
It - I wouldn't know.

HORACE
You signed the work order.

NEELY
My signature is on a great many things.

HORACE
This one is dated July thirtieth. Replace latch with exterior bolt. Reason given: theft prevention.

Silence. Miles stops writing. The boat creaks.

MILES
Where did you get that?

HORACE
From the contractor's invoice file. Delivered this morning, with a very nervous apology.

NEELY
He's lying.

HORACE
He's dead, Mr. Abbott. Since Tuesday. His widow brought the file.

Neely sets down the glass. It trembles against the saucer.

NEELY
Miles. Say something.

MILES
I'd like a recess.

HORACE
We are on a river, Mr. Kowalczyk. Where would you recess to?

MILES
The deck.

HORACE
Of course.

Miles stands, then sits again. He looks at Neely. Neely won't look at anyone.

NEELY
(quietly)
It was supposed to be empty.

MILES
Neely, don't -

NEELY
The insurance man said the engine room would be empty at night.

HORACE
And the eleven?

NEELY
They weren't on the schedule.

Horace finally uncaps the fountain pen. Writes one line. The nib scratches. Loud.

HORACE
That will do for today.

He caps the pen. The paddle wheel thuds. The lamps sway.
